;; Claudeworth Manor, the small household: the fresh world every
;; :claudeworth-small run starts from. A template: nothing writes to it.
;; Runs keep a copy (world-start.edn) and export their live map (map.edn) in
;; this format. A test bed for building a house out: the Barons of Claudeworth,
;; long poor, have come into a great legacy and moved into the one house the
;; title still owns, the old manor farmhouse, with a single servant. Everything
;; a proper house needs (rooms, furniture, fixtures, a kitchen fit to cook in,
;; servants) is still to get. The village and the abbey are the same places
;; and people as in the :claudeworth-medium world.
{:meta {:name "Claudeworth Manor"
        :meeting-place "front-yard" ; where candidates, guests, tradespeople and deliveries arrive
        :start "1904-10-18T08:00"
        :minutes-per-round 30
        :starting-funds 30000 ; whole pounds here; runtime money is old pence
        :setting-pack "settings/england-1904"
        :architect-prices "architect/prices-1904-medium.edn" ; the sizes and prices Pugh's firm quotes
        :setting "Claudeworth Manor is, for now, a name more than a house. The old hall burned in 1821 and its stone was sold; what the Barons of Claudeworth kept is the manor farmhouse beside the footings: four rooms down, two up, a leaking stable and an overgrown garden, let to tenants for fifty years and lately empty. The present baron, poor all his life, has come into a great legacy, and he and his wife moved in a week ago with one servant, meaning to make it a manor fit for the title. Claudeworth village lies by the gate, a sloping green with shops, a public house, a post office, a forge and tradespeople working from their cottages, as well as the offices of Pugh & Sons, Architects. Hollin Abbey is nearby too, where the monks keep to themselves."}

 :locations
 [;; ------------------------------------------------ the house (the old farmhouse, small and shabby)
  {:id "front-yard" :name "Front yard" :domain :house :floor :outside :kind :approach
   :aspects {:state "rutted gravel, weeds through it, a puddle by the door"}
   :connects-to ["entrance-passage" "stable-yard" "garden"]
   :desc "A rutted gravel yard before the farmhouse door, the lane running down to the village through a gateway with no gate in it."
   :detail "Two stone gateposts carry the Claudeworth crest, worn nearly smooth, and a broken gate lies in the nettles beside them. The house front is plain rubble stone under a sagging slate roof."}
  {:id "entrance-passage" :name "Entrance passage" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :hall
   :aspects {:cleanliness "mud tracked across the flags" :state "draughty, the door sticks"}
   :connects-to ["parlour" "kitchen" "staircase"]
   :desc "A narrow flagged passage inside the front door, the parlour on one side, the kitchen on the other and the stair going up at the back."
   :detail "Pegs along one wall for coats, a strip of matting worn to the string, and a damp stain climbing the plaster by the door."}
  {:id "parlour" :name "Parlour" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :sitting-room
   :aspects {:cleanliness "dusty, soot on the hearth" :state "the chimney smokes when the wind is west" :repair "a damp patch on the outside wall"}
   :desc "The one sitting room, low-beamed, with a small window on the yard. The family sit, write and dine here, for there is nowhere else."
   :detail "The wallpaper is a brown trellis pattern from the tenants' time, peeling at the corners. The floorboards dip towards the hearth, and the ceiling is low enough for a tall man to mind the beams."}
  {:id "kitchen" :name "Kitchen" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :kitchen
   :aspects {:cleanliness "greasy, the floor unswept" :state "cold"}
   :connects-to ["scullery" "larder" "back-room" "stable-yard"]
   :desc "A farmhouse kitchen with an old range, a deal table and a back door to the yard. The scullery, the larder and the back room open off it."
   :detail "Hooks in the beams where the tenants hung their bacon, a stone floor worn into hollows by the range, and one small window gone green at the edges."}
  {:id "scullery" :name "Scullery" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :scullery
   :aspects {:cleanliness "slimy round the sink"}
   :desc "A stone-floored lean-to with a shallow sink and the pump that is the house's only water."}
  {:id "larder" :name "Larder" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :pantry
   :aspects {:state "cool, a little damp"}
   :desc "A narrow north-facing larder with slate shelves and a meat safe. There is very little in it."}
  {:id "back-room" :name "Back room" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :bedroom
   :aspects {:state "cold, smelling of old milk" :cleanliness "swept"}
   :desc "The old dairy off the kitchen, whitewashed and stone-floored, where the butler has put up a camp bed. It is the only other room the servants have."}
  {:id "staircase" :name "Staircase" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :stair
   :aspects {:repair "the third tread cracked"}
   :connects-to ["landing"]
   :desc "A steep, boxed-in deal stair from the passage to the floor above, too narrow for a wardrobe."}
  {:id "landing" :name "Landing" :domain :house :floor :first :kind :landing
   :aspects {:cleanliness "dusty"}
   :connects-to ["best-bedroom" "lumber-room"]
   :desc "A cramped landing under the slope of the roof, with two doors off it."}
  {:id "best-bedroom" :name "Best bedroom" :domain :house :floor :first :kind :bedroom
   :aspects {:state "cold, the grate empty" :repair "a stain on the ceiling where the slates leak"}
   :desc "The larger room upstairs, over the parlour, which the baron and baroness share. It holds an old bed and very little else."
   :detail "A window over the yard and the lane, a small grate, and a crack in the plaster from the cornice to the floor."}
  {:id "lumber-room" :name "Lumber room" :domain :house :floor :first :kind :room
   :aspects {:state "crammed with crates" :cleanliness "dusty"}
   :desc "The second room upstairs, over the kitchen, filled with the crates and trunks the family brought. It could be a bedroom if it were cleared and furnished."}
  ;; ------------------------------------------------ outside
  {:id "stable-yard" :name "Stable yard" :domain :house :floor :outside :kind :yard
   :aspects {:state "nettles along the walls"}
   :connects-to ["stable" "garden"]
   :desc "A cobbled yard behind the kitchen, the old stable on one side and the privy against the far wall."}
  {:id "stable" :name "Stable" :domain :house :floor :ground :kind :stable
   :aspects {:repair "the roof open to the sky at one end" :state "empty, no horse or carriage"}
   :desc "A two-stall stable with a hay loft over it, empty these ten years. There is no horse, no carriage and no coach house."}
  {:id "garden" :name "Garden" :domain :house :floor :outside :kind :garden
   :aspects {:state "overgrown, brambles and long grass"}
   :desc "What was the hall's garden: a long slope of rough grass and brambles behind the house, with the grass-grown footings of the old hall at the top, where the land would take a much larger house."
   :detail "An old walled kitchen garden, its door off its hinges, has gone to docks and nettles; a few apple trees still stand in it. From the footings you can see the village roofs and the abbey tower."}

  ;; ------------------------------------------------ Claudeworth village (as in claudeworth-medium)
  {:id "village-green" :name "Village green" :domain :village :kind :green
   :connects-to ["general-store" "butchers-shop" "drapers-shop" "public-house" "post-office" "forge"
                 "sweeps-cottage" "barbers-cottage" "laundress-cottage" "labourers-cottage" "architects-office"
                 "bakers-shop" "poulterers-shop" "fish-stall" "fishmongers-cottage" "cobblers-cottage" "police-cottage"]
   :desc "A sloping green with a pump, an old market cross and geese. The shops face it."}
  {:id "general-store" :name "General store" :domain :village :kind :shop :hours ["08:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:general-store}
   :store-profile "A modest village general store in rural England, 1904. Everyday household necessities: groceries in small packages, dry goods, haberdashery, stationery, and simple domestic tools. These examples describe its scope, not an exhaustive inventory. Not a department store or specialist dealer. Only portable goods sold over the counter."
   :delivery-profile "Orders too large to carry away, and goods in the shop's line that can be got in from town, are sent up to houses in the neighbourhood."
   :courier :carrier
   :connects-to ["store-lodgings"]
   :desc "Groceries, candles, household necessities and gossip, sold over one long counter by Mr Briggs."
   :detail "Shelves to the ceiling of tins, jars and bolts of cloth, sacks of flour and meal along the floor, and a smell of paraffin and cheese. The bell over the door announces every customer, and the counter is where the village's news changes hands."}
  {:id "store-lodgings" :name "Shopkeeper's lodgings" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A snug room above the general store, with a brass bed and a kettle by the grate."}
  {:id "butchers-shop" :name "Butcher's shop" :domain :village :kind :shop :hours ["08:00" "18:00"]
   ;; :general-store enables the existing counter-and-quote service; the profile defines the trade.
   :services #{:general-store}
   :store-profile "A small village butcher's shop in rural England, 1904. Fresh beef, mutton, lamb and pork in ordinary household cuts, offal, sausages, bacon and bones for stock, wrapped for customers to carry away. These examples describe the trade, not an exhaustive inventory. No general groceries, household tools, live animals, whole carcasses or credit. Only portable purchases handed over the counter."
   :delivery-profile "Larger orders, more than a customer can carry, are sent up to houses in the neighbourhood."
   :connects-to ["butchers-lodgings"]
   :desc "Hobbs, Butcher: a small shop facing the green, with a scrubbed counter and joints hanging from hooks behind it."
   :detail "White tiles, a heavy chopping block, brass scales and a roll of wrapping paper. Mr Hobbs cuts and weighs meat at the counter; a cool back room keeps the day's stock out of the sun."}
  {:id "butchers-lodgings" :name "Butcher's lodgings" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "Mr Hobbs's rooms above the butcher's shop, with a plain bed and a sitting room overlooking the green."}
  {:id "drapers-shop" :name "Draper's shop" :domain :village :kind :shop :hours ["08:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:general-store}
   :store-profile "A village draper in rural England, 1904. Cloth by the yard, calico, flannel, dress lengths, household linen, ribbons, thread, buttons and other haberdashery, with a modest range of ready-made hosiery. These examples describe the trade, not an exhaustive inventory. Portable goods over the counter only; no bespoke dressmaking service, furniture or general groceries."
   :delivery-profile "Larger parcels in the drapery trade, too much for a customer to carry away, are sent up to houses in the neighbourhood."
   :connects-to ["drapers-lodgings"]
   :desc "Miss Ellis's drapery faces the green: a narrow shop with cloth measured out on a long counter and ribbons in the window."
   :detail "Shelves of folded calico and flannel rise behind the counter. A brass yard measure is let into its edge; drawers below hold buttons and small haberdashery. The window light is softened by lengths of lace curtain."}
  {:id "drapers-lodgings" :name "Draper's lodgings" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A neat bedroom above the drapery, with a patchwork coverlet and a window looking across the green."}
  {:id "public-house" :name "The Crown public house" :domain :village :kind :public-house :hours ["11:00" "22:00"]
   :services #{:general-store}
   :store-profile "A small fully licensed village public house in rural England, 1904. Beer, ale, porter, cider and modest measures of spirits, with bread and cheese or other simple cold refreshments. These examples describe its scope, not an exhaustive menu. Only portable food and drink handed over the bar; not a grocer, hotel or restaurant. No room bookings, rounds on credit or paid hospitality services."
   :connects-to ["publicans-room" "barmaids-room"]
   :desc "The Crown: a low, whitewashed public house, with a taproom opening from the green and a private stair behind the bar. The publican keeps it with a barmaid who lives in; there is no guest accommodation."
   :detail "Dark settles stand against the walls, the floor is flagged, and a small serving hatch opens towards the cellar stair. A painted crown hangs above the door; frosted lower panes screen the taproom from the green."}
  {:id "publicans-room" :name "Publican's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A plain bedroom above the Crown, with a washstand and a heavy woollen blanket on the bed. There are no guest rooms."}
  {:id "barmaids-room" :name "Barmaid's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A narrow attic room under the Crown's eaves, with an iron bedstead, a chest of drawers and a small square window over the yard."}
  {:id "post-office" :name "Post office" :domain :village :kind :office :hours ["08:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:general-store :post-office}
   :store-profile "A small village sub-post office. Edward VII postage stamps, stamped postcards, envelopes, writing paper and other modest postal stationery."
   :connects-to ["postmistress-room"]
   :desc "Mrs Bell keeps the sub-post office in the front room of a brick cottage. A red pillar box stands by the door, and a glazed counter separates callers from the sorting shelves."
   :detail "A portrait of Edward VII hangs above the counter. Pigeonholes line the back wall, with printed postal notices and a clock beside them."}
  {:id "postmistress-room" :name "Postmistress's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "Mrs Bell's quiet bedroom behind the post office, with a small grate and a curtained window onto the garden."}
  {:id "forge" :name "Blacksmith's forge" :domain :village :kind :workshop :hours ["07:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:blacksmith}
   :connects-to ["blacksmiths-room"]
   :aspects {:cleanliness "coal dust on the floor" :state "hearth banked"}
   :desc "Mr Carter's smithy stands at the edge of the green, its wide doors opening onto a shoeing space. He makes and repairs ironwork, tools and farm fittings as well as shoeing horses."
   :detail "A brick forge hood rises towards the tiled roof. The stone floor is dark with scale, and the doorway gives room to work on awkward lengths of iron. This is a village smithy, not a motor engineer's or a watch repairer's shop."}
  {:id "blacksmiths-room" :name "Blacksmith's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A small bedroom in the cottage adjoining the smithy, with a sturdy bed and a washstand by the back window."}
  {:id "sweeps-cottage" :name "Chimney sweep's cottage" :domain :village :kind :cottage :hours ["07:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:chimney-sweep}
   :connects-to ["sweeps-room"]
   :desc "Mr Rudge's cottage has a little front workroom where callers can arrange a chimney sweeping. He works with jointed rods and brushes, not climbing children."
   :detail "The limewashed workroom opens straight from the lane beside the green. A stone threshold and a dark patch by the back door show where sooty equipment is brought in; the sleeping room is kept apart from the work."}
  {:id "sweeps-room" :name "Chimney sweep's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A simple bedroom behind the sweep's workroom, with a narrow bed and a small chest of clean clothes."}
  {:id "barbers-cottage" :name "Barber's cottage" :domain :village :kind :cottage :hours ["08:00" "19:00"]
   :services #{:barber}
   :connects-to ["barbers-room"]
   :desc "Mr Webb works as a barber and hairdresser in the front room of his cottage. A striped pole marks the door; villagers come for a shave or a haircut."
   :detail "A mirror faces the window across a plain wooden chair with a headrest. The floor is bare boards, easily swept, and a little hearth warms the room. It is a small village barber's, not a fashionable ladies' salon."}
  {:id "barbers-room" :name "Barber's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A tidy bedroom upstairs at the barber's cottage, with a washstand and a faded print above the bed."}
  {:id "laundress-cottage" :name "Laundress's cottage" :domain :village :kind :cottage :hours ["07:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:laundry}
   :connects-to ["laundress-room"]
   :aspects {:cleanliness "flags scrubbed" :state "washhouse aired"}
   :desc "Mrs Dobbins takes in washing at her cottage, with a washhouse behind it and a small drying yard. Villagers bring bundles of linen to be washed, starched and ironed."
   :detail "The front workroom has space for sorting and folding linen; a rear doorway leads to the brick-floored washhouse. Lines cross the yard, and an airing rack hangs high under the washhouse rafters. There is no powered laundry machinery."}
  {:id "laundress-room" :name "Laundress's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A small bedroom upstairs, away from the steam of the washhouse, with a patchwork quilt and a chest beside the bed."}
  {:id "bakers-shop" :name "Baker's shop" :domain :village :kind :shop :hours ["07:30" "18:00"]
   :services #{:general-store}
   :store-profile "A village baker's shop with its own bakehouse in rural England, 1904. Bread baked on the premises each morning — cottage, tin and brown loaves, rolls and bread by the half-quartern — with buns, plain cakes, biscuits, and a little flour and yeast. These examples describe the trade, not an exhaustive inventory. No butcher's meat, general groceries, confectioner's fancy goods or meals. Only portable goods handed over the counter."
   :delivery-profile "Bread goes round to houses in the neighbourhood, and orders larger than a customer can carry are sent up."
   :connects-to ["bakers-lodgings"]
   :desc "Pocock, Baker: a bow-windowed shop facing the green, with the bakehouse behind it and a smell of warm bread across the grass in the early morning."
   :detail "Loaves stand in ranks on slatted shelves behind the counter, with a glass case of buns and cakes at one end and scales and a bread knife at the other. A half-door at the back opens on the bakehouse, where the brick oven is fired with faggots before dawn."}
  {:id "bakers-lodgings" :name "Baker's lodgings" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "Two rooms over the bakery, kept warm through the night by the oven below, with a plain bed and a window over the green."}
  {:id "poulterers-shop" :name "Poulterer's shop" :domain :village :kind :shop :hours ["08:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:general-store}
   :store-profile "A small village poulterer and licensed game dealer in rural England, 1904. Fowls, ducks, geese and turkeys, rabbits, new-laid eggs, and game in its season — pheasants, partridges, hares — plucked, drawn and trussed for the table, wrapped for customers to carry away. These examples describe the trade, not an exhaustive inventory. No butcher's meat, fish, general groceries or credit. Only portable purchases handed over the counter."
   :delivery-profile "Larger orders, more than a customer can carry, are sent up to houses in the neighbourhood."
   :connects-to ["poulterers-lodgings"]
   :desc "Garland, Poulterer and Licensed Dealer in Game: a narrow shop on the green, its window hung with birds and rabbits and a board of eggs on the sill."
   :detail "Fowls and rabbits hang in rows from a rail in the window, with a brace of pheasants among them in the shooting season. A marble slab and a pair of scales stand on the counter, and the plucking is done in a lean-to at the back, where feathers drift in the corners."}
  {:id "poulterers-lodgings" :name "Poulterer's lodgings" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A tidy bedroom over the poulterer's shop, with a feather bed, a rag rug and a window onto the green."}
  {:id "fish-stall" :name "Fish stall" :domain :village :kind :stall :hours ["08:00" "13:00"]
   :services #{:general-store}
   :store-profile "A fish stall on a village green in rural England, 1904, kept by a fish hawker who brings the morning's fish out from the railway station in the market town. Fresh sea fish — herrings, cod, haddock, plaice, whiting and soles — with kippers, bloaters and smoked haddock, and oysters, mussels or shrimps in their season, wrapped in paper for customers to carry away. These examples describe the trade, not an exhaustive inventory; what is to be had depends on what the morning brought. No meat, poultry, groceries or credit. Only portable purchases handed over at the stall."
   :desc "A fish stall by the market cross: a trestle under a canvas awning beside a hand-cart, where Mr Jessop sells the morning's fish until it is gone."
   :detail "Fish lie in rows on wet straw and rhubarb leaves on the trestle, with a tin scale hanging from the awning pole and a pail of water beneath. The hand-cart that brought it all from the station stands behind, its shafts down on the grass. The stall is packed away by early afternoon."}
  {:id "fishmongers-cottage" :name "Fishmonger's cottage" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A low cottage behind the green with a lean-to shed for the hand-cart, where Mr Jessop lives: a kitchen and a bedroom, with a smell of the trade that never quite leaves the back door."}
  {:id "cobblers-cottage" :name "Cobbler's cottage" :domain :village :kind :cottage :hours ["07:00" "19:00"]
   :services #{:cobbler}
   :connects-to ["cobblers-room"]
   :desc "Mr Kemp mends boots and shoes in the front room of his cottage beside the green; a boot painted on a board hangs over the door."
   :detail "A low bench stands under the window, with an iron last, a lapstone and a rack of awls and knives, and pairs of boots waiting their turn along the wall, each with a paper tag. The room smells of leather and cobbler's wax. It is a mender's workroom, not a boot shop: there are no ready-made boots for sale."}
  {:id "cobblers-room" :name "Cobbler's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A small bedroom behind the cobbler's workroom, with a narrow bed, a chest and his Sunday boots set square beneath the window."}
  {:id "police-cottage" :name "Police cottage" :domain :village :kind :office :hours ["07:00" "21:00"]
   :services #{:police}
   :connects-to ["constables-room"]
   :desc "The county police cottage at the end of the green, with a painted board reading COUNTY CONSTABULARY over the door. Constable Hollis lives here and receives callers in the front room."
   :detail "The front room is an office: a plain table and two chairs, a shelf of printed regulations and the occurrence book, and a notice board of swine-fever orders, lost property and wanted bills. A cape and a bull's-eye lantern hang on a peg by the door. There is no lock-up; prisoners are taken to the town."}
  {:id "constables-room" :name "Constable's room" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "The constable's bedroom upstairs at the police cottage, with an iron bedstead, a washstand and his tunic brushed and hung behind the door."}
  {:id "architects-office" :name "Architect's office" :domain :village :kind :office :hours ["09:00" "18:00"]
   :services #{:architect}
   :connects-to ["architects-lodgings"]
   :desc "Pugh & Sons, Architects: a front room of drawing boards, plan chests and shelves of reference books."}
  {:id "architects-lodgings" :name "Architect's lodgings" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :desc "Rooms above the office, comfortable and cluttered with rolled plans."}
  {:id "labourers-cottage" :name "Labourer's cottage" :domain :village :kind :bedroom
   :aspects {:cleanliness "flags scrubbed"}
   :desc "Two rooms and a scullery under a slate roof, one of a pair of cottages behind the green, where Alf lives with his mother and his sisters."}

  ;; ------------------------------------------------ Hollin Abbey
  {:id "abbey-gatehouse" :name "Abbey gatehouse" :domain :abbey :kind :gatehouse
   :connects-to ["abbey-church" "abbey-guest-house"]
   :desc "The old stone gatehouse of Hollin Abbey, its porter's bell on a chain. A painted board beside the arch directs visitors to the guest house, where the guestmaster receives them."}
  {:id "abbey-church" :name "Abbey church" :domain :abbey :kind :church
   :desc "Hollin Abbey's church, cool and quiet. The monks keep to themselves and sing the offices here; visitors who want a word with one of them ask at the guest house by the gatehouse."}
  {:id "abbey-guest-house" :name "Abbey guest house" :domain :abbey :kind :guest-house
   :services #{:hospitality}
   :connects-to ["guestmasters-cell"]
   :desc "A plain stone parlour inside the gatehouse court, where the abbey's guestmaster receives callers, pilgrims and anyone seeking counsel."
   :detail "A scrubbed oak table, a few rush-seated chairs and a crucifix on the whitewashed wall. A shelf holds a visitors' book, a Bible and a jug of water with two cups."}
  {:id "guestmasters-cell" :name "Guestmaster's cell" :domain :abbey :kind :bedroom
   :desc "A monk's bare cell off the guest house: a narrow bed, a prie-dieu and a single shelf of books."}]

 ;; journeys: [from to rounds], two-way; one round is half an hour
 :routes [["front-yard" "village-green" 1]
          ["front-yard" "abbey-gatehouse" 1]
          ["abbey-gatehouse" "village-green" 1]]

 ;; the positions people hold: the shared catalogue named in :meta :positions,
 ;; with these entries merged over it (see manor.mechanics.positions). Duties only:
 ;; who holds them and their situations are the agents'. The household is one
 ;; manservant where the house it means to become would keep a dozen servants.
 :roles
 {:baron
  {:title "Baron" :standing :family :sleeps :in-house :capabilities #{:appoints} :addressed-as "my lord" :ages [45 58] :sex :male
   :about "You are a peer and the master of the house. The house, the land and the household's money are yours to direct, and your servants act on your instructions. The money is a legacy, newly come: what the household has is what its account shows, and there are no rents or other income yet."}
  :baroness
  {:title "Baroness" :standing :family :sleeps :in-house :capabilities #{:appoints} :addressed-as "my lady" :ages [38 52] :sex :female
   :about "You are the lady of the house. You run the household and its servants: you order the meals and the house's daily life, give the servants their work and decide how the house should look and be furnished. You consult your husband about money."}
  :butler
  {:title "Butler" :standing :upper-servant :sleeps :in-house :capabilities #{:appoints} :addressed-as "Mr {surname}" :ages [45 62] :sex :male
   :about "You are the butler, and for now the only servant in the house, so you do every servant's work: answering the door, waiting at table, valeting the master, cleaning, fetching coal and water, lighting fires and, as best you can, cooking. You answer to the master and the mistress. As the household grows the other servants will answer to you."}
  :gm {:about "A small, shabby farmhouse in 1904 whose titled owners have come into a great deal of money and mean to make it a manor: their one servant, the village tradespeople going about their days, and a house to be built out, furnished and staffed. Household purchases are paid from the household account; there are no rents or other income, and no gains beyond it. Candidates and tradespeople arrive only through send-for after a recommendation; never invent or spawn new people."}
  :architect {:workplace ["architects-office"]}
  :shopkeeper {:workplace ["general-store"]}
  :butcher {:workplace ["butchers-shop"]}
  :draper {:workplace ["drapers-shop"]}
  :barmaid {:workplace ["public-house"]}
